what's up guys today we'll be developing  the mood board and material palette for  my filming room which I'm trying to turn  into a slightly more livable space  because right now it's a complete  disaster if you haven't watched the last  video where I developed the functional  layout for the space you can check it  out right here a portion of this video  is sponsored by Wayfair more on their  Awesome YouTube series later in this  video sometimes Architects really really  drill down on the functional aspects and  they skip this step altogether which is  why some architect design interior  spaces can feel really cold and  impersonal and actually I only learned  how to create a proper mood board during  my time when I was working with a lot of  interior designers but I would say this  is such an important process because you  can really narrow down on the material  and color palette and you can also  really learn a lot about your clients  and all of their hopes and dreams and  their weird desires  and  if you're doing it for yourself it can  really help you learn more about  yourself and what you're drawn to  because a lot of the times we just go  through our life saying oh I like this I  like this but we don't really fully  understand why so all in all a mood  board is really important in creating a  space that's yours and something that  reflects who you are so the very first  thing that I do is I just start coming  up with some words that describe the  mood that I'm trying to achieve the  interior designers that I worked with  they went Way Beyond and they created  like entire poems but I like to keep it  pretty nice and concise like six to nine  words mine were relaxing warm inviting  kind cozy uplifting and happy once I've  got these descriptive words I I start  scrounging through Pinterest to see  which images I'm drawn to they can be  images of Design Elements but not  necessarily they can be scenes from  nature or anything that evokes those  emotions that you're trying to create at  first don't be too precious with images  and just try to save all the images that  align with your words but as you're  doing this be mindful of your existing  conditions for example if you have any  signature architectural elements or  Furniture pieces that you want to keep  look for images that have that for  example I have a space with a fairly low  ceiling and little daylight so although  I'm really drawn to these dark and Moody  spaces I know it's going to be really  difficult to achieve because it is going  to feel very dark and very crammed I  also have this red brick which honestly  right now I'm going back and forth about  whitewashing is my red brick background  getting a little stale I don't know  are you guys sick of this  but if I do it there's no turning back  so let me know in the comments if I  should do this or not but anyways yes I  do have this right now and I do love  these warmer tones and it just reminds  me of autumn which is like my favorite  season so I'm pulling out a lot of  images with these warm red and orange  and brown tones I also know exactly what  flooring I want to use it's the Wickford  Oak vinyl plank flooring which I have in  my office and it's one of the few vinyl  plank products that really look and feel  like wood and obviously I would love to  use real wood but a vinyl is much more  affordable it's easier to install and  it's incredibly durable and it's  waterproof and since this is just my  filming room I am just gonna go with a  vinyl product so just based on these  parameters I already have my base color  palette and and so I'm pulling out a lot  of images that have elements of this to  get inspiration for complementary tones  and materials a quick message from  today's sponsor Wayfair  you guys probably know a Wayfarer as a  place where you can get Chic affordable  home product but you might not know  about their Awesome YouTube channel  they've got this cool new series called  interior motives where they try to solve  very specific design problems for  example having a really messy camera  setup like a certain somebody they might  bring in a professional videographer or  on this episode they're bringing in a  baker for tips on laying out a kitchen a  lot of times we design spaces based on  inspiration photos that we find on  Pinterest or on Instagram but to make  that space really functional and to make  it work for you we really need that  lived in experience of someone who uses  that space a lot especially when it  comes to knowing what storage to get  even if you're just trying to make your  space a little bit nicer or a little bit  more functional I think this series is  going to give you a lot of practical  tips and a lot of things to consider  anyways go check out their episode on  kitchen designs is really well produced  it's really well researched and it's  really fun to watch and go subscribe to  their channel it's full of really great  content I'll include the link in the  description so once you've got your  images you can start the selection  process but just be careful because with  Pinterest or Instagram it's really easy  to buy into a look but these images  are heavily fabricated and a lot of  times heavily photoshopped and it's  taken in a very specific spatial  condition so it can be really deceiving  because even if you copy exactly what's  in the image it's never going to look  the same in your space so when you're  narrowing down your selection try to  identify by what it is about the image  that you like for me I really love the  light neutral Mediterranean colors but  in this image I love how this darker  Walnut tone just makes it feel a little  bit more modern and sophisticated I'm  also really feeling these black details  because I think it just really elevates  the natural scheme here I really love  the combination of the fabric this color  glass Foss which again is a really nice  contrast but also adds something warm  and it also feels more contemporary and  elevated one thing to note I think a lot  of people feel like you need to get all  the pieces that are in the same style  category but you don't really need to as  long as they're in complementary color  tones don't be afraid of mixing and  matching especially if you're someone  who likes thrifting and having objects  that have like a story behind them the  this is going to add personality to your  space if you're having a hard time  understanding what you like a really  good reference can be your closet what  are the clothes that make you happy for  me my closet has a lot of black  red orange and neutrals lo and behold  those are the colors that I'm really  drawn to so try to see if there's any  color themes or textures or ideas that  you can pull from your closet if you see  a pattern then that's a pretty good  jumping off point because those are the  colors that you're going to feel good in  and of course you have your lived in  experience even though I love this  earthy texture I don't find them  comfortable for long periods like I had  these really soft pillows and I'd always  get compliments from people saying oh my  God this pillow is so nice and they'd  always hog the pillows and that  experiential quality is really important  for me so even if I like the look of  something I'd prefer to prioritize the  function so now that I've narrowed down  my mood board and I know what color  tones I want to use I gather the  physical material board I've got my own  material library at home but you can  also order samples from the manufacturer  or my go-to source is material Bank in  my last video I showed you how I  developed elevations by doing some quick  sketches and I was really focusing on  creating order in the space because it's  really kind of fragmented I did that by  creating a datum line along here and I  also wanted to create some height  because we have a very low ceiling  height so I did that by placing low  Furniture to bring th